    desc: "The alam is a freestanding metal standard, often two metres tall, carried at the head of Muharram processions in place of the standards of Imam Husayn's army. Its finial is typically the panja, a stylized open right hand, or a composition of crowns, fishes, tulips and calligraphic medallions hammered in brass, steel or gold-washed copper, sometimes inlaid with silver. Deccani workshops in Hyderabad and Bidri-influenced Lucknow produced the richest alams, many kept year-round in imambaras and ashurkhanas as objects of votive devotion, touched and crowned with cloth. On Ashura they are borne aloft through crowds beating chests to drumbeat, glinting above black flags. Museum collections from the V&A to the Salar Jung hold fine examples, and the craft of alam-making continues in hereditary family workshops.",
