    cat: "Music & Sonic Culture", c: "Sufi ritual music and dance", e: "regional_tradition", o: "Konya, Anatolia (13th century)", g: "Türkiye (Konya, Istanbul), Balkans, Syria",
    p: "founded 13th century; banned 1925; revived 1950s", era: "Seljuk Anatolia to Republican Türkiye",
    sum: "The Mevlevi Sufi ceremony in which semazens whirl in white flared skirts to ney flute, reed and drum as a moving meditation on union with the divine.",
    desc: "The sema is the Mevlevi order's whirling liturgy, founded by followers of the mystic poet Rumi in Konya, whose mausoleum remains the ceremony's heart. Semazens in white full-skirted tanbur-cloaks and tall felt sikke hats whirl in a choreographed circuit — arms open right palm up to receive, left palm down to bestow — while a flute, kudüm drum and rebab play the ayin suite and singers chant verses of Rumi. The ceremony proceeds through a greeting, the removal of the black cloak as burial shroud, four salams or whirl phases, and closing prayers, all under the sheikh's circulation around the hall. Banned with the dervish lodges in 1925, the sema survived as heritage and was proclaimed a UNESCO Masterpiece in 2005; Konya's December Seb-i Arus festival draws thousands to watch hundreds whirl.",
    col: [{ h: "#efe9dc", n: "whirling skirt white" }, { h: "#2b2620", n: "cloak black" }, { h: "#9c7b4d", n: "felt camel" }, { h: "#4b6b45", n: "reed green" }, { h: "#b89a5a", n: "lamp gold" }],
