    p: "14th century-1826; revival 1914-present", era: "Ottoman classical period",
    sum: "The world's oldest military band tradition — zurna shriek, kös giant drums and crescent standard-bearers marching the Ottoman army and terrifying European composers into imitation.",
    desc: "The mehterhâne was the Ottoman army's martial band, playing zurna shawms, boru trumpets, kös kettle-drums, davul, cymbals and triangles while halberd-bearing çavuşes marched with banners and the crescent alem standard, performing the gezik coordination step of striking right foot, knee and elbow. Its function was psychological as much as musical: thunderous tuning collisions and slow majestic peşrev marches announced siege lines and sultans, while European observers copied the sound into 'Turkish' style — Mozart's Rondo alla Turca and Beethoven's Turkish march descend from mehter's battery. Abolished with the Janissaries in 1826, the tradition was revived in 1914 and now performs in historical costume at the Istanbul Military Museum and state ceremonies, maintaining a repertoire of peşrev and mars form with unison singing in octaves.",
    col: [{ h: "#a01f24", n: "janissary red" }, { h: "#caa53d", n: "standard gold" }, { h: "#2d4a3a", n: "robe green" }, { h: "#e6d8bd", n: "turban white" }, { h: "#4a4a52", n: "halberd steel" }],
