    n: "Cappadocia rock-cut settlements", a: ["Göreme cave dwellings", "fairy chimney troglodyte towns", "Cappadocia cave architecture"],
    cat: "Architectural Style", c: "Troglodyte rock-cut architecture", e: "regional_tradition", o: "Byzantine Cappadocia (Anatolia)", g: "Nevşehir and Kayseri provinces, Türkiye",
    p: "4th century-20th century", era: "Byzantine to Ottoman Anatolia",
    sum: "Whole towns carved into volcanic tuff — dwellings, dovecotes, pigeon lofts and painted churches hollowed from Cappadocia's fairy chimneys and valleys.",
    desc: "In Cappadocia the soft volcanic tuff of eroded 'fairy chimney' cones could be cut with hand tools faster than it could be built with, so communities hollowed houses, stables, dovecotes, monasteries and entire underground refuges directly into the rock. Byzantine monastic communities carved rock churches in the Göreme valley whose vaults carry some of the finest 10th-12th century fresco cycles, including the Karanlık (Dark) Church; defensive multi-level refuges like Derinkuyu held thousands of people with ventilation shafts, wells and rolling stone doors. Vernacular dwellings continued the technique into the 20th century, dovetailing carved rooms with courtyards and pigeon houses whose droppings fertilised vineyards. UNESCO inscribed Göreme National Park and the Rock Sites of Cappadocia in 1985, and cave hotels now inhabit the same volumes.",
    col: [{ h: "#d8c3a0", n: "pale tuff cream" }, { h: "#b98d5f", n: "sunlit cone ochre" }, { h: "#8a5a3c", n: "cave interior brown" }, { h: "#7f9bb3", n: "highland sky" }, { h: "#c46a4a", n: "fresco terracotta" }],
