    sum: "A building culture in feather-light volcanic tuff whose rose, apricot and black shades give Armenian towns their monochrome glow and their seismic resilience.",
    desc: "The Armenian highland is sheathed in layers of volcanic tuff, a stone light enough to float yet weatherproof and easy to cut, and virtually all Armenian building — from medieval monasteries to 19th-century merchant houses — is made of it. Quarries yield different colours by region: the famous pink and apricot tufa of Yerevan and Artik, the black tuff of Gyumri, making whole districts monochrome in a single mineral tone. Masons laid precisely dressed ashlar in arch-and-vault systems long proven against earthquakes, carving cornices, rope-mouldings and khachkar-like ornament directly into the stone. Gyumri's Kumayri district preserves the largest historic quarter, its black-and-ochre houses with iron balconies built by the amira merchant class after the 1853 quake; Soviet-era Yerevan clad its monumental centre in pink tufa, earning the 'pink city' reputation.",
