    sum: "Fretted deep-veranda balconies in wild colour that wrap Tbilisi's older houses, mixing Georgian, Persian and neoclassical taste into a single signature facade.",
    desc: "The wooden balcony is the emblem of Tbilisi's historic architecture, built mostly in the 19th century when Russian imperial Tiflis grew with merchant houses on Sololaki, Betlemi and Abanotubani streets. Carpenters assembled deep verandas of turned posts, carved brackets, sunburst tympana and fine fretwork lattices, often glazed in later decades, then painted them in saturated colours — duck-egg blue, pistachio, oxblood — against plaster walls tinted ochre, rose or cream. The vocabulary braids Georgian carving, Persian timberwork and Italianate stucco order, with ironwork gates and shop awnings below. Decay in the 1990s led conservationists and programmes to restore hundreds of balconies; today they front guesthouses, embassies and the city's identity, and Kutaisi and Signagi carry related regional variants.",
