    p: "centuries-old; codified in 19th-20th century", era: "imperial and Soviet Georgia",
    sum: "The Georgian ritual feast presided over by a tamada toastmaster, where long toasts, polyphonic table songs and mountains of food turn a meal into a social institution.",
    desc: "A supra is the Georgian feast table: a spread of khachapuri cheese bread, mtsvadi grilled meat, pkhali walnut pastes, qvriviani herbs and clay-jar wine, presided over by a tamada or toastmaster who orders the evening through themed toasts — to ancestors, Georgia, women, friendship, the departed — each requiring a horn or glass emptied at length. Between toasts the table answers with polyphonic songs like Mravalzhamier, so the feast doubles as a concert, and tamadoba, the art of toastmaking, is judged as oratory. The supra runs from birthdays to funerals (a kelekhi mourning feast), making it the spine of Georgian social life across empires and Soviet decades. Ethnographers treat it as a living institution where hospitality, hierarchy and song are negotiated, and every Georgian film from Mimino to modern feasts stages its rules.",
    col: [{ h: "#7a1f2b", n: "saperavi wine red" }, { h: "#e6ddca", n: "linen table cream" }, { h: "#3d6b35", n: "herb pkhali green" }, { h: "#c78f3d", n: "bread crust gold" }, { h: "#5b4632", n: "clay jug brown" }],
