    p: "18th-19th century peak", era: "khanate-era Azerbaijan",
    sum: "Assembled-without-nails wooden window lattices of Shaki khanate palaces, filling glassless openings with geometric star patterns that cool and dim the light.",
    desc: "Shebeke is the Azerbaijani art of window lattices assembled from hundreds of small wooden elements — walnut, oak or poplar — joined by wooden pins and mortise-and-tenon alone, without glue or nails, a technique hereditary masters still guard. Openings were traditionally left unglazed: the lattice breaks wind and sun, dims harsh light into pattern, and lets rooms breathe in summer heat, while rich khans ordered screens inset with coloured glass and stained panes. The masterpiece is the Khan's Palace of Shaki (1797), whose hall windows run floor-to-ceiling in stained-glass-set shebeke that lights the mirrored interior in jewel tones. Craftsmen compute the star-and-rosette geometry by hand; UNESCO inscribed the Historic Centre of Sheki with the Khan's Palace in 2019, and shebeke restoration remains a living guild profession.",
    col: [{ h: "#6b4a2f", n: "walnut lattice brown" }, { h: "#e5d3ae", n: "strong light amber" }, { h: "#2f5d8a", n: "stained glass cobalt" }, { h: "#b8452f", n: "glass garnet" }, { h: "#d8c07a", n: "honey sunlight" }],
