    p: "medieval roots; canonised 19th-20th century", era: "khanate to Soviet Azerbaijan",
    sum: "A modal art music of tar, kamancha and daf where the singer navigates improvised ascents through dastgah modes, paralleling Persian dastgah and Arab maqam systems.",
    desc: "Mugham is Azerbaijan's classical modal music, built on dastgah modes such as Rast, Shur and Segah, performed by a trio of tar (plucked lute), kamancha (bowed spike fiddle) and daf frame drum supporting a khanende vocalist. A performance is an improvised ascent: the singer departs from a base section and travels through related modal sections, each demanding precise intonation and melodic knowledge, before returning — a journey often compared to the structure of Persian dastgah and Arabic maqam, its close relatives. Shusha in Karabakh produced legendary singers including Jabbar Garyaghdioglu and Bülbül, and Baku's mugham opera tradition fused the modes with staged drama. Soviet-era institutions preserved and codified mugham, and UNESCO proclaimed Azerbaijani Mugham a Masterpiece in 2003; the Baku Mugham Centre now stages international competitions.",
    col: [{ h: "#1f2a3a", n: "concert indigo" }, { h: "#c9a35c", n: "tar varnish gold" }, { h: "#8c2f2f", n: "curtain crimson" }, { h: "#d8cdb4", n: "programme cream" }, { h: "#4c6b52", n: "daf skin green-grey" }],
