    p: "medieval origins; 19th-20th century fame", era: "Caucasian khanate to Soviet era",
    sum: "A single Dagestani mountain village whose hereditary workshops filled museum vaults with densely engraved silver jugs, belts, daggers and fire-gilt niello work.",
    desc: "Kubachi, a stone village in mountain Dagestan, has practised metalwork for at least a thousand years; Persian chronicles praised Zirihgeran, 'the armour-makers', its medieval ancestor. Hereditary households divided tasks — one line for engraving, one for chasing, one for casting — and produced parade armour, chain mail, water vessels (kumgans), belts, pistol butts and kindjal scabbards in deep-relief chased silver, often gilded and blackened with niello, covered edge to edge in scrolling Arabic-style ornament and bird medallions. In the 19th century Kubachi work won Paris and Istanbul exhibition medals and entered the Hermitage and Kremlin Armoury collections; Soviet reorganisation made the village a state artel working through the 20th century. The craft survives in family workshops, and Kubachi ornament remains a Caucasian shorthand for dense hand-engraved silver.",
    col: [{ h: "#b9bec4", n: "chased silver grey" }, { h: "#c69c46", n: "fire gilding gold" }, { h: "#2a2a2e", n: "niello black" }, { h: "#6e4a2c", n: "walnut handle brown" }, { h: "#8e3b2e", n: "garnet inlay red" }],
