    desc: "Circassian male dress centres on the cherkeska, a tight-bodiced, long-skirted coat with sewn-on gazyr cartridge tubes across the chest, worn over a beshmet, with a narrow leather belt bearing silver fittings, soft kokej boots, a bashlyk hood and the tall wool papakha; the kindjal dagger hangs at the front in plain sight. Women wore the sa'i — a long dress with a high silver-buckled belt and a distinctive tall cap (dyshche paʼo) with veil, described by Ottoman and European travellers as graceful and courtly. After the 1860s genocide and mass exile to the Ottoman Empire, dress became a portable nation: diaspora troupes in Türkiye, Jordan and Israel kept the silhouette alive through dance costumes, while Soviet republics codified 'Caucasian' stage versions. The gazyr chest in particular became a pan-Caucasian icon copied by neighbouring peoples.",
