    sum: "The apricot-wood double-reed duduk whose breathy, mournful tone carries Armenian weddings, funerals and film scores, played in two-part drone-and-melody pairs.",
    desc: "The duduk is a short cylindrical pipe of apricot wood (tsiran, the Armenian tree) with a wide soft double reed, producing a warm, reedy, deeply mournful voice unlike any sharper oboe family instrument. It is played in pairs: one musician sustains the continuous drone (dam) while the duduk player improvises a melody in Armenian modal khaz notation heritage, ornamented with slides, trills and breath inflections. The repertoire spans dance tunes, love songs and laments — the funeral elegy krunk being the most famous — and the instrument accompanies both weddings and wakes, its timbre considered the sound of Armenian memory itself. Master Djivan Gasparyan carried it to global audiences and film soundtracks (Gladiator, Syriana), and UNESCO proclaimed 'Duduk and its Music' a Masterpiece of Intangible Heritage in 2005.",
