    sum: "Wine fermented and aged underground in enormous beeswax-lined clay qvevri, the oldest continuously practised winemaking method and source of Georgia's amber wines.",
    desc: "Qvevri are egg-shaped clay vessels up to two metres tall, lined inside with hot beeswax, buried to the neck in cellar earth so the surrounding soil keeps fermentation at steady temperature. Crushed grapes with skins, seeds and stalks go in whole, ferment on the skins through autumn and winter, and the wine draws amber colour, intense tannin and flavour from the long contact — the technique behind Georgia's distinctive amber (orange) wines from rkatsiteli and other varieties. Archaeology at sites like Gadachrili Gora traces the lineage back some 8,000 years to the earliest known winemaking, and every Kakhetian village still keeps marani cellars of buried qvevri tended by hereditary makers. Soviet industrialisation sidelined the method, but post-1990s revival made it the flag of Georgian identity and natural wine; UNESCO inscribed the tradition in 2013.",
